  "question": {
    "math_academy_id": "q-132145",
    "knowledge_point_id": "b903c40f-a2e5-5084-9c10-b2d33f423f1e",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Mean",
    "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2})$ with $P(X\\ge -1)=0.5793,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4207\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4602\\,$ | $\\,0.4562\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5000\\,$ | $\\,0.4960\\,$ | $\\,0.4920\\,$ |",
    "worked_solution": "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -1) & =P(X<-1) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge -1) \\\\  & =1-0.5793 \\\\  & =0.4207\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -1) & =0.4207 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{-1-\\mu }{5}) & =0.4207 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-1-\\mu }{5}) & =0.4207 \\\\ \\frac{-1-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.4207)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.2)=0.4207\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.4207)=-0.2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-1-\\mu }{5} & =-0.2 \\\\ -1-\\mu  & =-1 \\\\ \\mu  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
